The paper by Lutz Hendricks and Todd Schoellman examines the role of human capital in explaining cross-country income differences, using new data on pre- and post-migration wages of U.S. immigrants. They find that immigrants experience wage increases upon migration, but these increases account for less than half of the gap in GDP per worker between countries. This suggests that human capital plays a substantial role in these income differences.

Key methodologies include analyzing new data from the New Immigrant Survey (NIS) and additional sources like the Mexican Migration Project and the Latin American Migration Project. These datasets helped address challenges such as immigrant selection and skill transferability. By comparing immigrants' wages before and after migrating, the authors provide a more accurate measure of human capital's contribution to development accounting.

Specific metrics used in the analysis involve calculating the average wage gain at migration relative to the GDP per worker gap. For immigrants from countries with GDP per worker less than one-quarter that of the U.S., the wage gain at migration was found to be 38% of the total GDP per worker gap. This figure implies that physical capital and total factor productivity account for 38% of income differences, whereas 62% is attributed to human capital.

The paper also examines skill substitution, finding larger wage gains for less educated immigrants, indicating imperfect substitution between skilled and unskilled labor. They estimate the human capital share in development accounting to be between one-half and two-thirds, with a point estimate of 60% under certain assumptions.

Conclusions highlight that earlier studies using immigrant earnings likely underestimated the human capital share due to not accounting for immigrant selection and skill transfer. Mos
